POLITEHNICA Bucharest contributes to European higher education, research and innovation policy discussions through its participation in European associations, stakeholder platforms and expert communities, including CESAER, the European University Association (EUA), EELISA  Alliance, the EOSC Association and other European collaborative initiatives related to research, digital transformation and higher education.

University representatives and experts engaged in working groups, consultations, technical discussions and policy-oriented activities coordinated within these European structures. They have contributed as participants and co-authors to position papers, consultations and stakeholder contributions addressing European research, innovation, digital and higher education policies.

The university’s recent activities have included contributions related to:
•	European Research Area (ERA) Policy Agenda 2022–2024 and discussions concerning the future ERA Act, particularly regarding Open Science, research assessment, research security and dual-use research; 
•	activities within CESAER working groups related to open science, research security and dual-use technologies; 
•	European Open Science Cloud (EOSC) governance, interoperability, data sharing and open science policy development; 
•	Communication from the Commission on a European strategy for data; 
•	Digital Europe Programme initiatives concerning AI, cybersecurity, cloud infrastructures and common European data spaces; 
•	Proposal for a Regulation laying down harmonised rules on Artificial Intelligence (Artificial Intelligence Act); 
•	Proposal for a Regulation on a Single Market for Digital Services (Digital Services Act); 
•	Horizon Europe implementation and stakeholder discussions concerning the future Framework Programme for Research and Innovation (FP10); 
•	Multiannual Financial Framework (MFF) discussions concerning research, innovation and higher education priorities; 
•	European initiatives related to responsible internationalisation of research, research security and dual-use technologies; 
•	European cybersecurity initiatives linked to the activities of the European Cybersecurity Competence Centre (ECCC).

communication_activities:
POLITEHNICA Bucharest supported the European policy discussions through participation in CESAER, EUA and EOSC working groups, task forces, consultations and policy drafting activities.

University representatives contributed to position papers and expert discussions addressing:
•	Open Science policies; 
•	Research assessment reform; 
•	Research security and dual-use research issues; 
•	EOSC governance and implementation; 
•	Future FP10 and MFF priorities; 
•	AI, cybersecurity and digital transition policies; 
•	Advanced digital skills and engineering education. 

Experts from POLITEHNICA Bucharest have participated as contributors and co-authors in policy-oriented documents coordinated by European university associations and stakeholder platforms.
The university additionally participates in dissemination and stakeholder engagement activities related to European research, innovation and higher education policies, including workshops, conferences, consultation activities and collaborative European initiatives.

goals:
1) To carry out comprehensive activities for people living with spinal muscular atrophy and their families and carers, aimed in particular at combating exclusion, increasing independence, and improving the quality of life in the widest sense;

2) To build public awareness of spinal muscular atrophy, in particular through improving knowledge of genetics, diagnostics, standards of care, and therapeutic methods;

3) To improve the availability of diagnostic and treatment methods, rehabilitation techniques as well as technology solutions and products suitable for people affected by spinal muscular atrophy;

4) To support systemic solutions, particularly in the areas of public health and social support, that take into account the needs of people with spinal muscular atrophy and their near and dear ones.

