The Confederation of Trade Unions of the Slovak Republic (KOZ SR) engages with EU institutions and stakeholders on policies and legislative initiatives related to employment, social affairs and workers' rights. Its activities primarily concern:

implementation of the European Pillar of Social Rights and the European Pillar of Social Rights Action Plan;
the European Semester, particularly the employment and social policy recommendations addressed to Slovakia;
implementation and development of the Pact for Skills and the Union of Skills initiative;
EU policies on lifelong learning, vocational education and training, upskilling and reskilling of workers;
implementation of the European Social Fund Plus (ESF+) and other EU instruments supporting employment and social inclusion;
EU legislation concerning adequate minimum wages, working conditions, occupational safety and health, collective bargaining and social dialogue;
policies promoting equal treatment, non-discrimination, gender equality and work-life balance;
initiatives related to quality jobs, fair labour mobility, digitalisation of work, green transition and just transition.

communication_activities:
The Confederation of Trade Unions of the Slovak Republic (KOZ SR) carries out a broad range of communication and stakeholder engagement activities related to EU employment, social and skills policies. These activities include:

organising conferences, seminars, workshops and roundtable discussions on EU social, employment and labour market policies;
participating in consultations and structured dialogue with EU institutions and European social partners, particularly through cooperation with the European Trade Union Confederation (ETUC);
preparing position papers, policy recommendations and expert contributions on EU legislative and policy initiatives affecting workers and employers;
disseminating information on EU policies, funding opportunities and legislative developments through the KOZ SR website, newsletters, publications and social media channels;
implementing and communicating the results of EU-funded projects in areas such as skills, vocational education and training, lifelong learning, social dialogue, age management and labour market inclusion;
organising information events and public discussions through the Europe Direct Svidník information centre, promoting awareness of EU priorities, citizens' rights, employment, education and social policies;
cooperating with trade unions, employers' organisations, public authorities, educational institutions and civil society organisations to promote EU initiatives and facilitate stakeholder engagement.

These activities are carried out on a continuous basis throughout the year and are adapted to current EU policy priorities and legislative developments.

