communication_activities:
Throughout 2025, COMINROC—the Spanish Confederation of Extractive Industries of Industrial Minerals and Rocks—has continued to play a key role in representing and defending the collective interests of Spain’s industrial minerals and non-metallic extractive industries. Together with CONFEDEM, COMINROC forms PRIMIGEA, the representative platform of the Spanish minerals industry, which serves as the main interlocutor with national and European authorities on matters affecting the sector.
A significant part of COMINROC’s work during 2025 has focused on supporting the implementation of the European Critical Raw Materials Act (CRMA) and accompanying strategic projects promoted under its framework. Through its participation in PRIMIGEA, COMINROC has actively engaged with both Spanish and European authorities to ensure that the objectives of the CRMA are implemented in line with the original intentions of the Regulation, particularly regarding the strengthening of Europe’s strategic autonomy, the security of supply of essential raw materials, and the development of domestic extraction and processing capacities.
In June 2025, PRIMIGEA organised its Second Critical Raw Materials Forum at the headquarters of the Spanish Confederation of Employers' Organisations (CEOE) in Madrid. The event brought together representatives from the European Commission, Spanish public administrations, and industry stakeholders to assess the first year of implementation of the Critical Raw Materials Act and discuss its practical deployment in Spain. The forum provided a valuable platform for dialogue between policymakers and industry representatives on the opportunities and challenges arising from the new regulatory framework.
Following this event, COMINROC and PRIMIGEA have maintained numerous meetings and exchanges with national and European authorities aimed at facilitating the effective implementation of the CRMA, promoting strategic projects, and ensuring that the specific needs and realities of the industrial minerals sector are properly taken into account.
In parallel, COMINROC has continued to participate actively in a wide range of legislative and policy initiatives at both national and European level. During 2025, the Confederation has submitted comments, technical contributions, and policy recommendations on several important legislative files, including:
• Revision of the Public Procurement Directives;
• Circular Economy Act;
• Critical Raw Materials Act, including the Commission Implementing Act establishing the application template for Strategic Projects;
• Simplification of administrative burdens in environmental legislation;
• Proposal amending Regulation (EU) 2021/1119 on climate neutrality objectives;
• Measures aimed at reducing regulatory burdens for SMEs and mid-cap companies;
• Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism (CBAM);
• Industrial Decarbonisation Accelerator Act;
• GreenData4All initiative.
COMINROC’s advocacy and institutional activities are guided by a number of key principles that reflect the priorities of the sector:
• Sustainable and responsible management of mineral resources;
• Circular economy and resource efficiency;
• Legal certainty and regulatory stability;
• Industrial competitiveness and strategic autonomy;
• Occupational health and safety;
• Equality, skills development and talent attraction.
Through its work within PRIMIGEA, its cooperation with Spanish and European institutions, and its active participation in legislative and policy discussions, COMINROC remains firmly committed to ensuring a competitive, sustainable and resilient future for Spain’s industrial minerals and non-metallic extractive industries.

