eu_legislative_proposals:
PIPS engages with EU institutions and stakeholders on policies and initiatives relevant to Kosovo’s democratic development, European integration, good governance, rule of law, human rights, youth participation, social cohesion and civil society development.

Our engagement primarily focuses on EU policies and programmes concerning:

• EU enlargement and the EU–Western Balkans integration process, including the EU Enlargement Policy and the priorities relevant to Kosovo’s European integration;

• The EU’s Common Foreign and Security Policy and its engagement with the Western Balkans, particularly initiatives supporting peace, dialogue, regional cooperation and democratic resilience;

• Democracy, rule of law, fundamental rights and accountable governance, including EU policies and programmes supporting democratic institutions, citizen participation, transparency and institutional accountability;

• EU support for civil society and civic participation, including the Citizens, Equality, Rights and Values (CERV) programme and other EU funding and policy instruments supporting an enabling environment for civil society organisations;

• Youth policies and youth participation, including EU initiatives aimed at strengthening young people’s participation in democratic life, leadership development, civic engagement and social inclusion;

• EU policies related to reconciliation, social cohesion, conflict prevention and dealing with the past in the Western Balkans;

• EU policies and initiatives concerning media freedom, responsible journalism, access to information and the resilience of democratic public discourse;

• EU environmental and energy policies relevant to Kosovo, including the European Green Deal, the EU’s green transition agenda and policies related to energy transition, environmental governance and public accountability;

• EU programmes and initiatives supporting education, political education, capacity-building, skills development and cooperation among citizens and organisations across the Western Balkans.

PIPS does not seek to influence EU legislation on behalf of commercial interests. Its engagement is primarily aimed at contributing evidence, research findings and civil-society perspectives to policy discussions relevant to Kosovo and the Western Balkans, and at strengthening dialogue between policymakers, civil society, political actors, young professionals and other stakeholders.

Through meetings, policy discussions, public events, research and participation in relevant consultations and briefings, PIPS seeks to promote inclusive, evidence-based and accountable approaches to democratic governance and European integration.

goals:
From the Articles of ESPA
a. study questions relating to and defending the interests of the European Serum Processing Industry; 
b. represent its Members with the Commission of the European Communities, with other European institutions and with other national and international organizations whether in the private or the public sector;
c. promote the harmonization of regulations covering the trade and the use of animal serum, following international standards set by the World Animal Health Organization (WOAH) and the World Health Organization;
d. promote the education of Members, regulatory authorities and the serum industry stakeholders in general;
e. consolidate the confraternal ties between all of its Members; 
f. permit the exchange of professional and technical information within the industry itself but also with all economic and scientific sectors; 
g. ensuring that the market for serum products operates in compliance with the EU rules and with any international rules such as WOAH

