goals:
Consiglio Nazionale Forense (CNF), or Italian National Bar Council, is the highest institutional body representing the Italian legal profession.
Established by a 1933 Royal Decree-Law, its role has been reaffirmed by Law n. 247/2012 as the highest institution of the Italian legal profession and its governing and regulatory body.
Headquartered in Rome, within the premises of the Ministry of Justice, CNF has a central role in the legal system and a crucial social function - promotion of the rights protection - along with other several key functions, such as: 
- disciplinary (acting as anappellate body for disciplinary decisions and bar admissin matters); 
- Regulatory and Consultative to the Ministry of Justice on regulations affecting the legal profession and the justice system; 
- Educational oversight on lawyers training; 
- issues and enforces the Code of Conduct for Italian Lawyers.

