eu_legislative_proposals:
URI’s activities primarily concern EU legislative and policy frameworks in the fields of environmental protection, climate action, sustainable urban development, biodiversity, circular economy, sustainable mobility, good governance and civil society participation.

The main policies and frameworks addressed include:
-the European Green Deal;
-the European Climate Law and related climate mitigation and adaptation policies;
-the EU Strategy on Adaptation to Climate Change;
-the EU Biodiversity Strategy for 2030;
-the Nature Restoration Regulation;
-the Circular Economy Action Plan;
-the Zero Pollution Action Plan;
-EU legislation and policies concerning water quality, waste management, air quality, nature protection and environmental impact assessment;
-the EU Urban Mobility Framework and policies promoting sustainable and active mobility;
-the Green Agenda for the Western Balkans;
-the EU environmental acquis, particularly the requirements covered by Chapter 27 of the accession negotiations;
-the Charter of Fundamental Rights of the European Union;
-EU policies concerning democracy, the rule of law, transparency, institutional accountability and civil society participation;
-the Aarhus Convention principles concerning access to environmental information, public participation in decision-making and access to justice.

URI follows the development and implementation of these policies, particularly as they relate to Albania and the Western Balkans. Its activities focus on supporting the alignment of national and local policies with EU requirements, strengthening institutional capacity and promoting the effective application of environmental, climate and governance standards.

URI contributes through research, policy analysis, technical recommendations, stakeholder consultations, public awareness activities, capacity building and participation in civil society platforms, expert meetings and policy discussions. Its engagement is based on evidence generated through research and project implementation and aims to support transparent, inclusive and evidence-based policymaking.

communication_activities:
It is AEPI's firm belief that strong parliaments – in their role as arbiters of peace, stability and prosperity – lie at the heart of long-term development and the realisation of human rights. Accordingly, we look to work in partnership with EU institutions to strengthen African parliamentary institutions and promote human dignity by supporting democracy on the continent. It is these activities and partnerships that AEPI wishes to continue dialogue with the European Commission, European Parliament and other related institutes.  
Built on the legacy of long-tested solidarity and cooperation between African and European parliamentarians, as its name suggests, AEPI is a joint African-European initiative with a network of former and current parliamentarians from both continents. AEPI believes that Good Governance and well-functioning democratic institutions are vital for the well-being of a country's citizens, and ultimately for a continent. A crucial role of parliamentarians is to ensure their governments deliver on their responsibilities to their citizens and are held accountable. 
AEPI believes that the capacity of a group of current and former parliamentarians from across Europe acting together to strengthen the voice for Africa in Europe, and to work to assist the practical tasks of African Parliamentarians, is important, both for Europe and Africa. To this end, we continue to strengthen and build our network throughout Europe. As our core business is peer-to-peer parliamentary exchange, we draw upon our network of European parliamentarians to represent AEPI in activities online or in person with our African partners, where mutual exchange can take place.
We aim to build AEPI Parliamentary Groups (APG’s) in the European Parliament and national parliaments. These groups would consist of like-minded sitting and former parliamentarians who share the vision, and are, or want to work for, stronger ties between our two continents. These groups would: 
•	Advocate for Africa in Europe;
•	Organise Africa-related parliamentary events;
•	Receive African delegations to their parliament;
•	Support our projects in Africa;
•	Follow programmes, especially if their parliament is an AEPI donor;
•	Attend AEPI events where relevant and possible in Europe or Africa. This could be, for example, the local APG, thematic cross-European or African (virtual) meetings and activities.

