eu_legislative_proposals:
The Foundation for Access to Rights (FAR) works on issues related to the implementation and development of European Union legislation and policies concerning migration, asylum and the protection of fundamental rights.
In particular, FAR’s activities relate to the legislative framework established under the Pact on Migration and Asylum, adopted by the European Union in 2024, which introduced a comprehensive reform of EU migration and asylum legislation and established new rules governing asylum procedures, migration management, border procedures and responsibility-sharing between Member States.
Relevant legislative instruments include in particular:
Regulation (EU) 2024/1351 on Asylum and Migration Management, establishing rules on responsibility for examining applications for international protection and solidarity mechanisms between Member States;

Regulation (EU) 2024/1348 establishing a common procedure for international protection in the Union;

Regulation (EU) 2024/1356 introducing the screening of third-country nationals at the external borders;

Regulation (EU) 2024/1358 establishing the Eurodac system for the comparison of biometric data for the effective application of asylum and migration policies;

Directive (EU) 2024/1346 laying down standards for the reception of applicants for international protection;

Regulation (EU) 2024/1359 addressing situations of crisis and force majeure in the field of migration and asylum.

These legislative instruments are directly relevant to FAR’s work. The organisation provides legal assistance to migrants, refugees, asylum seekers and stateless persons in Bulgaria; conducts research and policy analysis; develops digital tools facilitating access to legal information and services; and engages in advocacy and awareness-raising activities related to the practical implementation of EU asylum and migration legislation.
Through its legal aid work and field experience, FAR contributes practical expertise regarding the functioning of asylum procedures, reception conditions, access to legal remedies and the social inclusion of beneficiaries of international protection. This experience informs policy discussions concerning the implementation of EU migration and asylum legislation and the protection of fundamental rights.

