Regulation (EU) 2023/1805 on the use of renewable and low-carbon fuels in maritime transport (FuelEU Maritime), particularly the recognition and uptake of renewable hydrogen and hydrogen-derived maritime fuels;

Directive (EU) 2023/2413 amending the Renewable Energy Directive (RED III), particularly the production and use of renewable hydrogen and renewable fuels of non-biological origin;

Regulation (EU) 2024/1735 establishing a framework of measures for strengthening Europe’s net-zero technology manufacturing ecosystem (Net-Zero Industry Act), particularly its application to electrolysers, fuel cells, offshore renewable-energy technologies and related infrastructure;

Directive 2014/89/EU establishing a framework for maritime spatial planning, particularly the treatment of multipurpose offshore renewable-energy, hydrogen-production and alternative-fuel infrastructure;

The EU Strategy on Offshore Renewable Energy (COM(2020) 741 final) and the Commission Communication “Delivering on the EU offshore renewable energy ambitions” (COM(2023) 668 final), particularly policy support, permitting and grid or port integration for multipurpose floating offshore renewable-energy installations.

The EU Ports Strategy (COM(2026) 112 final), particularly the role of ports as clean-energy hubs, the deployment and safe handling of renewable hydrogen and alternative fuels, port-centred energy communities, offshore renewable energy integration, permitting and access to finance for innovative port infrastructure;

The European Ocean Pact (COM(2025) 281 final), particularly the development of a competitive and sustainable blue economy, offshore and ocean energy, sustainable aquaculture, maritime spatial planning, the greening of ports and shipping, and support for coastal and island communities.

The Sustainable Transport Investment Plan (COM(2025) 664 final), particularly investment support for renewable and low-carbon maritime fuels, renewable hydrogen and related port infrastructure.

The organisation’s activities concern the development of an enabling EU policy and regulatory framework for floating, multipurpose offshore platforms combining renewable-energy generation, green-hydrogen production, storage and refuelling infrastructure, including their integration with ports, islands and maritime transport. Relevant subject matters include permitting, maritime spatial planning, technical and safety standards, access to EU funding, demonstration-project support and the classification of floating hydrogen infrastructure within EU energy, transport and maritime policies.

