Le attività del Centro Studi e Formazione Professionale E.S.S.E. sono rivolte, in particolare, alle seguenti iniziative e politiche dell'Unione europea:

European Skills Agenda for sustainable competitiveness, social fairness and resilience (COM(2020) 274 final), con particolare riferimento allo sviluppo, aggiornamento e riconoscimento delle competenze professionali e all'apprendimento permanente;
Council Recommendation of 16 June 2022 on a European approach to micro-credentials for lifelong learning and employability (2022/C 243/02), con particolare interesse per la qualità, trasparenza e riconoscibilità delle competenze acquisite attraverso percorsi formativi e professionali;
Council Recommendation on vocational education and training (VET) for sustainable competitiveness, social fairness and resilience (2020/C 417/01), con riferimento alla formazione professionale, all'aggiornamento delle competenze e alla relazione tra formazione e mercato del lavoro;
European Qualifications Framework for lifelong learning (EQF), quale quadro europeo per favorire trasparenza, comparabilità e trasferibilità delle qualifiche;
Council Recommendation on Individual Learning Accounts (2022/C 243/03), con particolare riferimento all'accesso alla formazione continua e allo sviluppo professionale;
European Education Area – Strategic framework for European cooperation in education and training towards the European Education Area and beyond (2021–2030), con particolare attenzione all'apprendimento permanente, alla mobilità e alla valorizzazione delle competenze;
European Pillar of Social Rights Action Plan (COM(2021) 102 final), in particolare per gli aspetti riguardanti formazione, competenze, occupazione e inclusione sociale;
Social Economy Action Plan – “Building an economy that works for people: an action plan for the social economy” (COM(2021) 778 final), con particolare riferimento allo sviluppo dell'economia sociale, delle competenze e delle organizzazioni della società civile.

Il Centro Studi segue tali iniziative con particolare riguardo alla qualificazione delle professioni, alla formazione permanente, alla certificazione e trasparenza delle competenze, alla mobilità professionale e alla valorizzazione delle professioni non organizzate in ordini o collegi, nonché al ruolo delle associazioni professionali e delle organizzazioni della società civile nei processi europei.

communication_activities:
Communication and engagement activities related to the EU policy areas described above will primarily consist of direct one-to-one engagement with relevant European policymakers and stakeholders, with a particular focus on Romanian Members of the European Parliament.

Depending on the specific client mandate and policy file, Public Affairs Solutions may facilitate and support in-person meetings in Brussels, Strasbourg or Romania, although a significant proportion of the engagement is expected to take place through online meetings and videoconferences with Romanian MEPs, their advisers and other relevant stakeholders. Such meetings are intended to present the position of represented organisations, provide relevant industry information and evidence, discuss the potential impact of EU legislative initiatives, and contribute constructively to ongoing policy debates.

Where relevant, engagement may also involve representatives of the European institutions, national authorities involved in EU policymaking, industry associations, business organisations, experts, civil society organisations and other stakeholders relevant to a particular legislative or policy initiative.

In addition to direct meetings, activities may include:

- organisation of policy conferences, roundtables, stakeholder dialogues, workshops and business events in Romania or Brussels;
- participation in conferences, hearings, policy forums and institutional or industry events related to EU legislation;
- preparation and distribution of position papers, policy briefs, one-pagers, background documents, legislative assessments and recommendations;
- coordination of industry, association or coalition positions on relevant EU regulatory matters;
- communication campaigns aimed at raising awareness of specific policy issues;
- stakeholder mapping and targeted outreach to policymakers and relevant experts;
- organisation of study visits, site visits, company visits or meetings designed to provide policymakers with a better understanding of particular sectors and investments;
- preparation of briefing materials for meetings with MEPs and other institutional stakeholders;
- media relations and strategic communications supporting public affairs objectives, where appropriate;
- organisation of public or closed-door discussions involving policymakers, industry representatives, experts, professional associations and other relevant stakeholders.

Public Affairs Solutions has extensive experience in organising stakeholder engagement activities in Romania and the CEE region. Previous activities have included policy conferences and roundtables involving government officials, parliamentarians, industry representatives, healthcare professionals, patient organisations and other stakeholders. Examples include the organisation of the first conference in Romania dedicated to biosimilar medicines, which contributed to dialogue between the Ministry of Health, the National Health Insurance House, pharmaceutical industry representatives and patient associations, as well as a Parliamentary roundtable focused on generic and biosimilar medicines involving political, institutional, medical and patient stakeholders.

The agency has also coordinated official openings, high-level business events, press field visits, media breakfasts and inauguration ceremonies, including the inauguration of a major generic and biosimilar manufacturing facility in Turda, Romania, involving engagement with senior public-sector stakeholders.

For EU-related mandates, these capabilities may be used to facilitate informed dialogue between represented organisations and European policymakers, particularly Romanian MEPs, and to ensure that relevant sector expertise, economic considerations and practical perspectives are communicated transparently during the EU policymaking process.

goals:
The purpose of the organization is to contribute to the realization of the human rights set forth in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and the European Convention on Human Rights through:
a) the promotion of education and public awareness regarding the situation in Georgia (Section 52 (2) No. 7 AO);
b) the promotion of assistance for politically persecuted individuals and for refugees in and from Georgia (Section 52 (2) No. 10 AO).

eu_legislative_proposals:
ATS Advisory’s activities primarily relate to the preparation and implementation of the EU Multiannual Financial Framework (MFF) 2028–2034, including the legislative, policy and programming framework governing EU funding instruments and programmes under the next MFF.

Further areas of interest include EU legislation, policies and funding frameworks related to:

the European Green Deal and the EU’s climate and decarbonisation objectives;

energy efficiency, renewable energy, electrification and the energy transition;

the energy performance and decarbonisation of buildings;

sustainable and low-emission transport and mobility;

alternative fuels and related transport and energy infrastructure;

the decarbonisation and energy efficiency of industry and SMEs;

circular economy, resource efficiency and other measures supporting the EU’s green transition; and

EU funding and financing instruments supporting investments in these areas.

