i)	finding and recommending environmentally sustainable and practical solutions for domestic, municipal, industrial and agricultural waste (including wastewater) management in Ireland;
ii)	lobbying Government and local authorities to implement environmentally sustainable waste management practices, including clean production, elimination of toxic substances from products, re-use, recycling, segregation of discarded materials at source, the Circular Economy, and other beneficial practices;
iii)	lobbying Government to follow the best international practice in other countries, regions and cities ;
iv)	raising public awareness about the long-term damaging human and animal health and economic consequences of landfilling and of the destruction of materials by incineration; and,
v)	maintaining contact and exchanging information with similar national networks in other countries, and with international zero waste organisations.

communication_activities:
Many submissions to Irish Government Departments and Agencies; lectures to organisations such as the Food Safety Authority of Ireland, the Sustainability Summit, Dublin, etc.
Submissions include:

i)	Lecture on “How to Eliminate Single Use Plastic from our Lives”, to a seminar at Green Earth Organics, County Galway (http://zerowasteireland.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/04/PlasticsGalway260818.pdf); 
ii)	Lecture, by invitation, to the national Construction Industry Summit, Dublin, Ireland, on Climate Change and Waste: “How the Construction Industry can Survive in a World of Zero Waste and Climate Change”; March 2019;
iii)	Lecture, by invitation from the European Union Office to Hong Kong and Macao, on the topic of “Addressing the Plastic Waste Challenge in Europe – Learning from Experience”; Hong Kong, March 2019;
iv)  Submission to the Department of Communications, Climate Action and Environment in response to a public consultation on the proposed introduction of new environmental levies, December 2019;
v)  Submission to the Department of Communications, Climate Action and Environment, providing observations on a new Waste Action Plan for Ireland as part of the transition to a Circular Economy, February 2020;
vi)  Submission on Significant Water Management Issues (SWMI) in Ireland, August 2020.
vii) Submission to the European Commission on State Aid, in response to the Aarhus Convention Compliance Committee complaint (October 2022);
viii) Submission responding to public consultation on Ireland's energy security (October 2022)
ix) Submission on Ireland’s Fourth National Biodiversity Action Plan (November 2022);
x) Presentation on “Water and Sustainability – A Necessary Alliance”, at the All-Ireland Water & Wastewater Summit, November 2022;
xi) Several presentations on transforming the construction industry so that it could become climate neutral; and,
xii) Submissions on the separation, recovery and reuse of the phosphorus and nitrogen content of wastewater (2019 to 2022).

On-going activities include maintaining an active and frequently updated web page (http://zerowasteireland.com/) a Facebook page (https://www.facebook.com/ZeroWasteAllianceIreland/) and a Twitter account (https://twitter.com/zerowaster) as a way of raising public awareness of the Zero Waste approach, Zero Waste news and activities (in Ireland and other EU Member States), and reaching out to our supporters and members of the public.
In recent years we participated in two pilot-scale projects: (i) conversion of discarded food items (“food waste”) into a usable and safe compost for horticultural use; and (ii) recycling of used but hard-to recycle metallised plastic film crisp packets.  These projects are currently on hold, but may be reactivated in the future.

ZWAI is represented on the Irish Government’s Waste Forum and on the Government’s Water Forum (An Fóram Uisce) by one of the ZWAI board members (Ollan Herr), and ZWAI is a member of the Irish Environmental Network (IEN), and the Environmental Pillar.  Another board member (Jack O’Sullivan) is a founder member of the Climate Change Committee of An Taisce, and is a member of the Climate Research Co-ordination Group, established under the Environmental Protection Agency’s (EPA) 2014–2020 Research Strategy.

In 2019 ZWAI became a full member of the European Environment Bureau (EEB); and we intend to participate (as far as our resources will allow) in the development of European Union policy on waste and the Circular Economy.

goals:
The strategic objective of the Alliance is to foster the development of a global market for powerfuels. The Alliance has three main goals:

1. Raise awareness and acceptance of powerfuels as an indispensible component of reaching global climate targets and achieving climate neutrality;
2. Support the further enhancement of regulatory frameworks with a first focus on Europe as a lead market and major demand region;
3. Stimulate project development to globally enable production capacities on an industrial scale, thus achieving cost competitiveness with fossil fuels.

