communication_activities:
The activities of PTBN include, among others: 
• the annual PTBN Report: "Terrorist threats and the anti-terrorism system in the Republic of Poland", developed by the PTBN Problem Analysis Team;
• educational initiatives implemented by social media (#PTBNedu - educational cards of PTBN, #ZaleceniaAT (CT Recommendations) - recommendations for improving the anti-terrorism system of the Republic of Poland); 
• webinars on the state security carried out in cooperation with the Centre of Studies and Education for Security (CSES) of the University of Wrocław; 
• lectures, interviews, training for critical infrastructure operators, students of civic initiatives and high school students; 
• cyclical patronage over the prestigious National Congress: "Antiterrorism security of public buildings - SafePlace".

The latest events: On the 10th of September 2021, the PTBN and the CSES of the University of Wrocław hosted a conference entitled "The 20th anniversary of the war on terrorism - balance and prospects", the aim of which will be to summarize two decades of efforts in the fight against terrorism at the national and international level. The event was held under the honorary patronage of, among others by the National Security Bureau, the Ministry of the Interior and Administration, the Government Center for Security, the Civil Aviation Authority and the Institute for Western Affairs. Zygmunt Wojciechowski and the participation of the US Embassy in Poland. 

Previous initiatives of public debate regarding the area of ​​security carried out by PTBN - independently or in cooperation with other entities:

- Participation in the work of the "Task Team for the development of security standards for Critical Infrastructure Operators in the field of prevention, response and reduction of the effects of threats posed by unmanned systems" established under the aegis of the RCB (Governmental Security Centre) and the plenipotentiary of the Minister of Infrastructure for drones and with the participation of a number of state institutions and external experts.

- PTBN together with DG HOME of the European Commission organized trainings dedicated to the protection of places of religious worship against terrorist attacks, under the honorary patronage of the Head of the National Security Bureau. On March 14, 2022, a training was conducted for representatives of the Union of Jewish Religious Communities in the Republic of Poland with the support of the Center for Crisis Protection and Management of the European Jewish Congress. On May 13, 2022, training for representatives of the Catholic Church was conducted. The trainings were also attended by representatives of the Police, the State Protection Service and local authorities responsible for crisis management and public order.

- In December 2021, members of PTBN presented the our Reports during a meeting of the Policy Group on Protection of Public Spaces led by DG Home of the European Commission.

- In December 2021, PTBN provided a contribution to the United Nations Office of Counter-Terrorism report entitled "Terrorist attacks on the basis of xenophobia, racism and other forms of intolerance, or in the name of religion or belief." The report will be presented at the 77th session of the UN General Assembly in September 2022.

- In October 2021, PTBN became a member organization of the expert working group on drones led by DG MOVE of the European Commission.

goals:
Law reform: Elevating standards of animal welfare with a law reform that extends current anticruelty laws by adding positive animal welfare law.

Positive animal welfare law (“PAWL”):
(a) reflects the contemporary scientific model of the Five Domains model;
(b) is consistent with the existing legal paradigm which functions on human legal responsibilities regarding animals;
(c) pragmatically elevates applied standards of animal care by (i) retaining existing duties to prevent cruelty; and (ii) adding duties to provide animals with opportunities to experience positive states;
(d) was first legislated in 2019 (Australia) and is replicable in all jurisdictions throughout the EU.

PAWL is created in two simple steps:
(a) explicitly recognising animals as sentient
(b) Legislatively defining “sentience”  stating “sentience means that animals experience negative and positive [physical, mental and emotional] states”.
See https://sentientanimallaw.org/law-reform

